entry stanislaw-ulam
term: Stanislaw Ulam
difficulty: advanced
related_terms: edward-teller
related_terms: nuclear-fusion
related_terms: j-robert-oppenheimer
related_terms: yield
quick_facts: Idea man behind the Teller–Ulam H-bomb design (1951)
quick_facts: Co-invented the Monte Carlo method with von Neumann
quick_facts: Manhattan Project mathematician at Los Alamos
quick_facts: Designed Project Orion's nuclear-pulse propulsion concept
quick_facts: Survived leukemia thanks to one of the first chemotherapy successes

sources:
    {"organization":"Atomic Heritage Foundation","title":"Stanislaw Ulam","url":"https://www.atomicheritage.org/profile/stanislaw-ulam"}

definition:
    Polish-American mathematician (1909–1984) who cracked the hydrogen bomb's central problem. In 1951 he proposed using radiation from a fission explosion itself to compress and ignite fusion fuel — the 'radiation implosion' insight that became the Teller–Ulam design behind every thermonuclear weapon since. A Manhattan Project veteran, he also co-invented the Monte Carlo method with John von Neumann over playing cards, pioneered early computing applications, and sketched nuclear-pulse propulsion for Project Orion.

debunking_snippet:
    “Teller alone invented the hydrogen bomb.” The breakthrough — compressing fusion fuel with X-rays from a fission trigger — came from Ulam's calculations; Teller ran with it. Both names belong on the design.
